<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Toda of Pamplona</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 885–after 958</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Toda Aznárez, Toda of Navarre</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Toda of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a> was queen consort of Sancho I Garcés of Pamplona and regent for their son García Sánchez I. Through her network of marriages she was linked to most of the royal houses of Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and she was aunt of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-iii">Abd al-Rahman III</a>. In 958 she travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> with her grandson, the deposed King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-i-of-leon">Sancho I of León</a>, to seek the caliph's help.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/toda-of-pamplona.jpg" alt="Toda of Pamplona" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ultrasiete · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Inscripci%C3%B3n_reina_Toda.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Toda was the daughter of Aznar Sánchez of Larraun, a paternal grandson of King García Íñiguez of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a>, and of Onneca Fortúnez, a daughter of King Fortún Garcés, so she descended from the earlier royal dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inigo-arista">Íñigo Arista</a>. She married King Sancho I of Pamplona. When he died while their son García was still a child, he was succeeded by his brother Jimeno Garcés, the husband of Toda's sister Sancha.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On King Jimeno's death in 931 Toda became regent and guardian of García Sánchez I. In 934 she signed a treaty pledging allegiance to her nephew Abd-ar-Rahman III and released hostages of the Banu Di n-Nun clan, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> confirmed García's rule, a step sometimes read as the caliph's way of freeing García from his mother's control. A count Fortún Garcés rebelled at Falces and was put down with Cordoban arms. Toda broke the treaty in 937, bringing a punitive campaign.</p><p>She remained powerful long after the regency. She appears in royal charters to the exclusion of her daughter-in-law the queen from 947 to 955, and in 958 she ruled a subkingdom of her own around Deio and Lizarrara. She arranged political marriages for her daughters across Christian Iberia: Urraca and Onneca became queens of León, and Sancha married Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fernan-gonzalez-of-castile">Fernán González of Castile</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In 958 Toda sought help for her grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-i-of-leon">Sancho I of León</a>, whose obesity was largely responsible for his loss of the throne. Abd-ar-Rahman III sent his Jewish physician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasdai-ibn-shaprut">Hasdai ibn Shaprut</a>, who promised a cure if Toda came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>. She arrived with García, Sancho, nobles and clergy and was received with full honours. The visit of a Christian queen to the caliphal capital raised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>'s prestige among his subjects and is considered a landmark of medieval diplomacy, and Sancho's treatment succeeded.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Toda of Pamplona” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 958 the deposed King Sancho I of León, known as ‘the Fat’, travelled to Córdoba with his grandmother Queen <b>Toda of Navarre</b> to be treated for obesity by Hasdai ibn Shaprut.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caliphate-of-cordoba">Caliphate of Córdoba · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>In 958 the elderly Queen <b>Toda of Pamplona</b> travelled to Córdoba with her grandson Sancho the Fat of León, who was treated there by the caliph’s physician for his obesity.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-navarre">Kingdom of Navarre · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.82,-1.65&amp;year=932" aria-label="Pamplona on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Toda of Pamplona"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4897 3912h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4897 3912h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.82,-1.65&amp;year=932">What was here in 932 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
